1
00:00:01,408 --> 00:00:07,358
This is Milestone 10, iteration one, of my disaster response
coordination capstone.

2
00:00:07,358 --> 00:00:09,795
A note on how this video was made.

3
00:00:09,795 --> 00:00:19,903
The voice you hear is a computer voice, and a script types
and runs each command you see, against the real application
and the real database.

4
00:00:19,903 --> 00:00:26,928
I, Ben, reviewed this script before recording, and I will
watch this recording before I submit it.

5
00:00:27,929 --> 00:00:30,197
Here is what the software does.

6
00:00:30,197 --> 00:00:31,953
A field report comes in.

7
00:00:31,953 --> 00:00:42,491
An agent classifies how serious it is, reserves a real unit
for it, and then either approves the assignment on its own,
or sends it to a person.

8
00:00:42,491 --> 00:00:46,515
Everything runs on the real database, with seeded data.

9
00:00:47,015 --> 00:00:57,043
First, one command builds a fresh database and loads the
seed: one Medical agent, exactly two Medical units, and a
stand-in dispatcher.

10
00:00:58,045 --> 00:01:03,853
Now I start the application in the background, with the
reservation clock turned on.

11
00:01:04,853 --> 00:01:07,817
A health check, to show it is up.

12
00:01:08,318 --> 00:01:08,941
Report A.

13
00:01:08,941 --> 00:01:13,373
Someone fell and has a leg injury, and is conscious and
talking.

14
00:01:13,373 --> 00:01:20,020
The response shows the severity, Serious, a confidence of
ninety-five, and the recommended unit.

15
00:01:20,020 --> 00:01:30,614
Because confidence is seventy-five or more and the severity
is not Severe or Critical, the system approved it by itself,
and a notification was recorded.

16
00:01:32,615 --> 00:01:34,008
The database agrees.

17
00:01:34,008 --> 00:01:46,827
The proposal row shows the confidence, the model, which is a
rules-based stand-in called fake rules, because the real
Ollama model is not wired in yet, and that it was decided by
auto.

18
00:01:47,828 --> 00:01:52,237
Unit one is now assigned, and unit two is still available.

19
00:01:52,738 --> 00:01:57,661
A notification row was written when the assignment became
final.

20
00:01:57,661 --> 00:01:58,815
Sent at is set.

21
00:01:58,815 --> 00:02:03,584
Acknowledged at is empty, because acknowledgment is not
built.

22
00:02:03,584 --> 00:02:07,354
To be clear, notify here means a database record.

23
00:02:07,354 --> 00:02:09,969
No message leaves the machine yet.

24
00:02:10,970 --> 00:02:12,399
Report B is vague.

25
00:02:12,399 --> 00:02:15,574
Something happened, nobody is answering.

26
00:02:15,574 --> 00:02:22,401
The confidence comes back at forty, under seventy-five, so
the system does not decide.

27
00:02:22,401 --> 00:02:26,608
The decision stays empty and it is held for a person.

28
00:02:28,608 --> 00:02:34,546
While it waits, its unit is reserved, and anyone using the
software can see the clock.

29
00:02:34,546 --> 00:02:40,623
This shows exactly when each reserved unit will be freed,
and how many seconds are left.

30
00:02:40,623 --> 00:02:46,630
After four minutes without a decision, the unit is released
and the proposal times out.

31
00:02:47,630 --> 00:02:49,753
I want to say this out loud.

32
00:02:49,753 --> 00:02:51,421
There is no login yet.

33
00:02:51,421 --> 00:02:59,608
Anyone who can reach this address could press approve, and
it always counts as the stand-in demo dispatcher.

34
00:02:59,608 --> 00:03:02,792
Real login is planned before Milestone 12.

35
00:03:02,792 --> 00:03:06,052
This is a known, accepted gap for the demo.

36
00:03:07,052 --> 00:03:10,551
Here is the queue of proposals waiting for a person.

37
00:03:11,052 --> 00:03:13,710
The dispatcher approves proposal two.

38
00:03:13,710 --> 00:03:23,409
The decision is recorded against the dispatcher's id, not
auto, so the audit trail tells the two paths apart, and the
unit is notified.

39
00:03:25,410 --> 00:03:27,110
Now the failure path.

40
00:03:27,110 --> 00:03:31,158
Report C needs a Medical unit, but both are taken.

41
00:03:31,158 --> 00:03:33,263
The system does not guess.

42
00:03:33,263 --> 00:03:40,548
It answers with a four hundred and nine, unit already
reserved, and nothing is dispatched.

43
00:03:42,549 --> 00:03:44,255
Finally, the test suite.

44
00:03:44,255 --> 00:03:57,766
It covers the safety rule, the reservations, the database
rules, and the two extra slices, the offline queue and
Override Assignment, which I am showing through their tests
rather than live.

45
00:03:58,767 --> 00:03:59,999
What is not done.

46
00:03:59,999 --> 00:04:05,075
Real dispatcher login, the scenario reader, and the real
Ollama model.

47
00:04:05,075 --> 00:04:14,647
The classifier you saw is a rules-based stand-in, and the
five second speed requirement is not met until the real
model is measured.

48
00:04:14,647 --> 00:04:18,853
Those are tracked, with Milestone 11 and 12 as the target.

49
00:04:19,854 --> 00:04:21,910
That is iteration one.

50
00:04:21,910 --> 00:04:26,022
The repository is tagged iteration dash one.

51
00:04:26,022 --> 00:04:26,957
Thank you.
